WEBVTT

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.359
Is recursive self-improvement a computational process?

00:00:04.858 --> 00:00:14.798
A machine redesigns itself. The new machine does the same. We can draw the arrow from each to the next. The question is which way it points.

00:00:16.598 --> 00:00:20.540
Start small, with an old procedure for the square root of two.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.755
Treat your guess as one side of a rectangle of area two.

00:00:25.255 --> 00:00:28.979
Average the two sides, and divide the area by the result.

00:00:30.179 --> 00:00:30.780
Again.

00:00:33.479 --> 00:00:37.449
And again. The rectangle is now very nearly a square.

00:00:38.549 --> 00:00:44.250
But what entitles us to call each guess better than the last? The arithmetic does not say.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:55.406
Here, a proof does. Each new guess must land between the root and the old one. The arithmetic takes the step. The proof says which way is forward.

00:00:56.806 --> 00:01:01.709
Now replace the instruction to average with an instruction to build a better machine.

00:01:02.209 --> 00:01:07.409
The sides of the rectangle told us which guess was better. What does that work here?

00:01:09.409 --> 00:01:14.235
First, a distinction from Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs.

00:01:14.735 --> 00:01:20.037
Two procedures for adding three and four. Each is written with a call to itself.

00:01:21.087 --> 00:01:24.812
Run the first. Increments pile up, waiting.

00:01:25.312 --> 00:01:27.479
Then it unwinds, to seven.

00:01:29.712 --> 00:01:35.952
Run the second. It moves one unit across at each call, and nothing is left waiting.

00:01:36.452 --> 00:01:39.908
Stop it anywhere, and two numbers are enough to resume.

00:01:40.408 --> 00:01:44.469
Both procedures are recursive. Only the first process is.

00:01:46.069 --> 00:01:50.791
The difference can be expensive. This procedure fits on an index card.

00:01:51.291 --> 00:01:59.524
Watch the process it generates. Dark circles are new questions. Pale ones are the same questions, computed again.

00:02:00.024 --> 00:02:08.179
Forty-one calls, for eight distinct questions. The size of a procedure tells us little about the time its process will take.

00:02:09.829 --> 00:02:16.244
Now, a machine that revises itself. What must you save, to stop it and start it again?

00:02:16.744 --> 00:02:22.711
The model, the editor, the selection rule, the data. Call the whole package p.

00:02:23.211 --> 00:02:30.738
One round of revision is a single step, from one package to the next. The editor is part of what it rewrites.

00:02:31.238 --> 00:02:33.221
Run it again, and again.

00:02:33.721 --> 00:02:37.095
The whole run, however long, is one program.

00:02:37.595 --> 00:02:40.349
Recursive: the product goes back in.

00:02:40.849 --> 00:02:43.334
Self: what the editor edits.

00:02:43.834 --> 00:02:51.517
Improvement needs a comparison, and the step contains none. The loop can run before anyone says what better means.

00:02:53.367 --> 00:02:58.866
Can a machine improve itself, and prove it? Under a fixed standard, yes.

00:02:59.366 --> 00:03:05.477
Pay it the integer it returns. The first machine erases its input, and returns zero.

00:03:06.377 --> 00:03:11.657
The editor keeps that program, and appends one instruction: add one.

00:03:12.157 --> 00:03:14.020
The new machine returns one.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:19.847
Let the editor work on its own product. Two. Three. Four.

00:03:20.777 --> 00:03:25.479
On every input, the machine at round n returns exactly n.

00:03:25.979 --> 00:03:30.955
So each successor earns strictly more than the last. That is a theorem.

00:03:31.905 --> 00:03:35.033
The proof can travel on the same disk as the editor.

00:03:38.405 --> 00:03:40.662
Our editor's machine came with a proof.

00:03:41.162 --> 00:03:46.508
Keep the standard exactly as it is. Now a stranger hands in a program.

00:03:47.008 --> 00:03:52.284
Will it return more than six on every input? Same question, different source.

00:03:52.784 --> 00:03:59.527
The inspector may read it, run it, or search for a proof. It must finish, with yes or no.

00:04:01.327 --> 00:04:07.241
No such inspector exists. Take any inspector, and write a program with it in hand.

00:04:07.741 --> 00:04:12.378
The program runs the inspector on its own text, and then does the opposite.

00:04:13.228 --> 00:04:19.116
If the verdict is yes, it behaves like a program that fails. The yes was a mistake.

00:04:20.366 --> 00:04:26.198
If the verdict is no, it behaves like a program that passes. The no was a mistake.

00:04:26.697 --> 00:04:31.177
Any inspector that always answers is wrong about some program.

00:04:32.827 --> 00:04:39.947
So be more generous. Let the inspector publish a verdict every morning, and take it back whenever it likes.

00:04:40.897 --> 00:04:46.166
It need only settle, some day, on the right answer. This program defeats it.

00:04:46.666 --> 00:04:53.899
On input x, it waits for a no, on day x or later. Then it answers as a good program would.

00:04:54.649 --> 00:04:57.060
Suppose the verdicts settle on yes.

00:04:57.561 --> 00:05:00.841
Input zero finds a no at once, and answers.

00:05:01.341 --> 00:05:04.649
Inputs one and two find the no at day two.

00:05:05.149 --> 00:05:11.786
But input three starts after the last no. It reads yes, and yes, and never stops.

00:05:12.286 --> 00:05:14.246
So does every later input.

00:05:14.746 --> 00:05:19.670
Finitely many answers cannot meet the standard. So yes is wrong.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.854
Now suppose they settle on no.

00:05:23.354 --> 00:05:27.292
Then every input finds one, and every input is answered.

00:05:27.792 --> 00:05:32.832
The program behaves exactly like the good one. So no is wrong.

00:05:33.782 --> 00:05:37.011
And never settling breaks the inspector's one promise.

00:05:38.511 --> 00:05:45.468
One standard, two results. Along the editor's path, every successor passes, with proof.

00:05:45.968 --> 00:05:51.168
Across all programs, no computable inspector can settle the question.

00:05:51.668 --> 00:05:57.264
Not by deciding. Not by confirming, or refuting. Not even in the limit.

00:05:57.764 --> 00:06:00.939
The work is done by one word: all.

00:06:02.789 --> 00:06:08.333
Could we avoid choosing a standard, and ask for a successor at least as good on every task?

00:06:08.833 --> 00:06:15.580
One task pays a point for agreeing with the incumbent. Any successor that differs loses a point somewhere.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:19.887
So at least as good on everything means the same behavior.

00:06:21.537 --> 00:06:25.482
Better, then, needs weights. Here the successor leads.

00:06:25.982 --> 00:06:29.604
Add one task with enough weight, and the ranking reverses.

00:06:30.104 --> 00:06:33.520
Composing the suite decides what the competition is for.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:41.526
One more machine. It certifies true claims that a program never halts on its own text.

00:06:42.026 --> 00:06:47.859
A sound certifier always misses one true claim: the claim about itself.

00:06:48.358 --> 00:06:51.083
So a successor can add it, automatically.

00:06:51.583 --> 00:06:55.110
Round after round, the books gain a true certificate.

00:06:56.783 --> 00:06:59.821
Now gather every round into one certifier.

00:07:00.321 --> 00:07:08.257
It misses its own claim too. Strict improvement, forever, and one truth the whole path never reaches.

00:07:09.357 --> 00:07:17.277
Seen from above, the same instruction comes round. Seen from the side, it is a spiral, and it rises.

00:07:17.777 --> 00:07:21.212
Here, we can name the coordinate along which it rises.

00:07:23.112 --> 00:07:27.344
A laboratory reports a score that rises, round after round.

00:07:27.844 --> 00:07:32.570
Four histories fit that curve. A cheaper way to the same performance.

00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:34.604
A test that was learned.

00:07:35.104 --> 00:07:37.466
An examiner that changed with the candidate.

00:07:37.966 --> 00:07:40.606
Or evidence that came in from outside.

00:07:42.356 --> 00:07:48.127
To test the word recursive, compare an early editor with a late one, on equal terms.

00:07:48.626 --> 00:07:52.377
Does the later editor buy more improvement for the same search?

00:07:53.997 --> 00:07:55.528
What will be held fixed?

00:07:56.028 --> 00:07:58.368
What should a later generation do better?

00:07:58.868 --> 00:08:01.270
What finding would show it had done worse?

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:07.686
Until then, we know how to set the machine going. We still have the surveyor's question to answer.
